  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 230 PM EDT Sat Aug 8 2026\n\nThru Tonight...Storms focused near the coast this morning and\nearly afternoon but should now focus more on the interior and \nVolusia county through early evening. HREF (ARW and NSSL) is not \nbullish with development as the HRRR and RRFS are. But given the \ndeep moisture present across the north, sfc heating and eventual \nboundary interactions (not to mention active storms in NE FL), \nthink additional storm development looks likely with the highest \ncoverage focusing through the I-4 corridor and Lake county. Have \nnot seen much wind with the earlier storms near the coast but \ncannot rule out a wet downburst of 40-50 mph. Of greater concern \nare the frequent lightning strikes (including a few \"bolts from \nthe blue\") seen earlier and should redevelop with additional\nstorms.\n\nA risk of heavy to excessive rainfall will exist due to the very\nmoist airmass in place as 15Z XMR sounding shows PWAT of 2.27\".\nStorm motion is slower and the deep southerly flow is encouraging\ncell segments to train over the same area at times. Quick \nrainfall totals of 2-3\" with isolated spots to around 5\" are \npossible, leading to temporary flooding issues. Convection will \ngradually wind down after sunset across the interior, with mostly \ndry conditions overnight.\n\nSun...Atlantic ridge axis will begin to shift southward tomorrow,\nwith onshore winds out of the east-southeast forecast. Aloft, a \nmid-level ridge will begin to nudge its way in from the east and \ncombine with the advection of drier air aloft (PW values falling \nto 1.8-2.0\"). So this should lead to a decrease in showers and \nstorms. However, still expecting scattered coverage (PoPs 30-50%)\ninto the afternoon/evening, especially across the interior. Storm\nthreats of frequent lightning, strong wind gusts and locally \nheavy rainfall will continue with any stronger convection. Hot and\nhumid conditions persist, with highs in the low 90s, and \npotentially reaching the mid 90s northwest of I-4. Peak heat index\nvalues still forecast around 102-107F in the afternoon. \n\nMon-Sat...Ridge aloft continues to build west and across Florida,\nremaining in place across the region through all of next week. \nThis will lead to continued hot and humid conditions and also \ncombine with drier air moving in aloft to produce below normal \nstorm coverage each day. Afternoon/evening shower and storm \nchances drop to 20-30% Monday and Tuesday and 20% or less \nWednesday-Friday. Atlantic ridge axis shifting southward and \neventually south of central FL into mid/late week will also \neventually switch the prevailing flow offshore, which will put an\nend to any potential for isolated onshore moving showers during \nthe overnight/early morning hours. \n\nWith limited relief from afternoon convection, heat impacts will \nrise into next week, with highs near to above normal in the low to\nmid 90s leading to a widespread Moderate to Major HeatRisk. Peak \nheat index values will continue to solidly reach 102-107F, but for\nportions of the area could see these values nudge toward Heat \nAdvisory criteria of 108-110F, especially Tuesday onward. Will \ncontinue to monitor the need for any Heat Advisories next week.",
